<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/12th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Second Battle of Tarain</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1192">1192</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Second Battle of Tarain was fought in 1192 near Taraori in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haryana">Haryana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, a year after the first. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-of-ghor">Muhammad of Ghor</a>, using <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mounted-archer">mounted archers</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feigned-retreat">feigned retreats</a>, defeated the much larger army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prithviraj-chauhan">Prithviraj Chauhan</a>, who was captured and later executed. The victory opened northern India to Ghurid conquest and led to the founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-battle-of-tarain.jpg" alt="Second Battle of Tarain" width="320" height="199" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Allan Stewart · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_last_stand_of_Rajputs_against_Muhammadans.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prithviraj-chauhan">Prithviraj Chauhan</a>'s forces had defeated the Ghurids on the same field at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-battle-of-tarain">First Battle of Tarain</a> in 1191, after which Mu'izz al-Din, seriously injured, returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazni">Ghazni</a> and prepared to avenge his defeat. The size of the armies is uncertain. The 16th–17th-century writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firishta">Firishta</a> gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chauhan">Chauhan</a> army 3,000 elephants and 300,000 cavalry and infantry, figures that modern historians such as Satish Chandra and Kaushik Roy regard as exaggerated, while Minhaj-i-Siraj credits Mu'izz al-Din with 120,000 armoured men. The Hammir Mahakavya and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prithviraj-raso">Prithviraj Raso</a> say that only part of Prithviraj's army was on the field.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>According to Hasan Nizami's Taj-ul ma asir, Prithviraj advised Muhammad Ghuri to return home, and Ghuri replied that he would ask his brother, the reigning ruler, to approve a peace in which the Ghurids would take Tarhind, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multan">Multan</a>. Nizami and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firishta">Firishta</a> make clear that this was a deception, which Prithviraj accepted as a genuine truce. The Ghurids, formed into five units, attacked before sunrise while the Chahamana army had passed the night in slumber and merry-making.</p><p>Minhaj describes 10,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mounted-archer">mounted archers</a> in four divisions sent to surround the enemy on four sides, with orders not to engage when attacked but to fall back and wear out the elephants, horses and infantry. A fifth unit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feigned-retreat">feigned retreat</a>, drawing the Chahamana troops into a charge that a fresh unit of 12,000 cavalry then repelled. When the remaining Ghurid forces attacked, the Chahamana troops fled in panic.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Prithviraj left his elephant, fled on horseback and was captured near Sursuti. Most medieval sources say he was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajmer">Ajmer</a>, where Muhammad planned to reinstate him as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>, and was killed for treason after rebelling. The Ghurids subjugated the whole Chahamana territory and placed his son Govindaraja IV on the throne of Ajmer. Prithviraj's younger brother Hariraja dethroned Govindaraja and recaptured part of the kingdom, but was defeated by the Ghurid general Qutb al-Din Aibak, and the Ghurids went on to defeat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayachandra">Jayachandra of the Gahadavala dynasty</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chandawar">Battle of Chandawar</a> and conquer northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Second Battle of Tarain” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Qutb ud-Din Aibak, a Turkic slave-general who had governed the Ghurid conquests in northern India from Delhi since the victory at <b>Tarain</b> in 1192, assumed independent power at Lahore and founded the Mamluk or Slave Dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1191 a Rajput coalition under Prithviraj Chauhan defeated him at the First Battle of Tarain, but the next year, at the <b>Second Battle of Tarain</b>, his mobile cavalry won a decisive victory.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghurid-sultanate">Ghurid Sultanate · Military</a></li></ul></section>
